 \subsection{Compared Architectures and Exact Fusion Formulations} F1 averages calibrated probabilities: $p=(p_v+p_s)/2$. F2 fits a fixed-$C=1$ L2 logistic stacker, \begin{equation}p=\sigma(b+a_vp_v+a_sp_s).\end{equation} It has three scalar parameters. Its signed coefficients are unconstrained and do not form convex reliability weights. F3 instead projects the 128-dimensional visual representation and 220 sensor statistics to separate 128-dimensional vectors, concatenates and layer-normalizes them, and uses a hidden layer of width 32 or 64. Candidate training budgets are ten or twenty epochs.
 F4--F6 use a masked softmax gate and calibrated branch logits: \begin{equation}w=\operatorname{softmax}(h(u)+\text{availability mask}),\qquad p=\sigma\!\left(\sum_m w_m\operatorname{logit}(p_m)\right).\end{equation} F4 receives four action indicators and two availability indicators. F5 receives thirty-six quality descriptors and availability; F6 receives all forty-two inputs. Hidden widths are eight or sixteen. The gates have no final intercept correction or post-gate calibrator. Context or quality can encode process content as well as reliability; interpreting a learned weight as causal sensor trust is therefore unsupported.\ev{Exact implemented fusion links}{\EvidenceRef{E066}{}}
