+\section{Study Synthesis and Remaining Questions} The clearest result was the improvement in failure ranking from sensor statistics alone to the logistic stacker. The paired comparison with equal averaging remained inconclusive. Without a sensor-only model given the same second-stage calibration, the gain cannot be attributed to the camera alone. Outer-test recordings remained independent of fitting and threshold selection, although reuse of the outer-training OOF bank limited independence during inner stacker validation. The original adaptive gates showed no general advantage. When absence was known, routing returned the surviving branch's prediction. Performance under corruption showed why detecting an unreliable stream that is still present is a separate problem.
+The three favorable follow-ups changed different parts of the pipeline. Last-quarter pooling used the end of the completed action. Anchored adaptation improved ranking but reduced recall and increased calibration error relative to its anchor. Longer PatchTST training improved the temporal sensor predictor but left it below sensor statistics alone; the two sensor representations performed better together. No run combined the three changes. Choosing components after comparing their results on the same recordings would compound selection bias: the choices could reflect idiosyncrasies of that cohort. A joint pipeline would need to be fixed in advance and evaluated on untouched recordings. The augmentation-matched and quality-interaction comparisons left their primary robustness contrasts unresolved. Generalization to natural failures and shifts, independently adjudicated labels, new seeds and predictions made before action completion also remains open.
